PROMPT_CASE_RUN explain status=Completed error=None result=Some("**Result: `first_lines` does not return \"the first `MAX_LINES` lines\" — it returns `MAX_LINES + 1` items, and it panics on short inputs.**\n\n## What it returns\n\n`src/limit.rs` contains:\n\n```rust\npub const MAX_LINES: usize = 3;\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    (0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n\n- The range is `0..=MAX_LINES`, which is **inclusive**: `0, 1, 2, 3` — that is 4 iterations.\n- With `MAX_LINES = 3`, the function therefore returns a `Vec<String>` with **4 elements**: `lines[0]` through `lines[3]`, each cloned into an owned `String`.\n- If the intent was \"first 3 lines\", the inclusive bound is an off-by-one bug; `0..MAX_LINES` (or `(0..=MAX_LINES).map(...)` with `MAX_LINES = 2`) would be needed.\n\n## What happens for short inputs\n\n- If `lines` has **fewer than 4 elements**, `lines[i]` indexes a slice out of bounds, so the call **panics** with \"index out of bounds: the len is N but the index is i\" — there is no bounds check, no `get()`, no `take()`, and no `Vec` truncation.\n- Concretely: an empty slice or one with 1–3 elements panics (at the first index ≥ `len`); a slice of exactly 4 or more elements succeeds.\n- The only \"graceful\" case is any input with at least 4 items, where it always returns exactly 4 owned strings regardless of total length.\n\nNo files were changed.")
